Since you prefer practicing directly in the chat, here are 5 "tricky" questions that focus on the core logic of Inheritance, Variable Hiding, and Static Binding.
Try to solve these without an IDE!
Question 1: The "Hiding" Variable
class Alpha {
    int x = 10;
}
class Beta extends Alpha {
    int x = 20;
}
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Alpha a = new Beta();
        Beta b = new Beta();
        System.out.println(a.x + " " + b.x);
    }
}

A) 10 20
B) 20 20
C) 10 10
D) 20 10
Question 2: Static Method "Shadowing"
class Parent {
    static void display() { System.out.println("Parent"); }
}
class Child extends Parent {
    static void display() { System.out.println("Child"); }
}
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Parent obj = new Child();
        obj.display();
    }
}

A) Child
B) Parent
C) Compilation Error
D) Runtime Exception
Question 3: The Constructor Trap
class Base {
    Base() { show(); }
    void show() { System.out.println("Base"); }
}
class Derived extends Base {
    int x = 100;
    void show() { System.out.println("Value: " + x); }
}
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new Derived();
    }
}

A) Base
B) Value: 100
C) Value: 0
D) Compilation Error
Question 4: Multi-level Shadowing
class A { int i = 1; }
class B extends A { int i = 2; }
class C extends B { int i = 3; }

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        A obj = new C();
        System.out.println(((B)obj).i);
    }
}

A) 1
B) 2
C) 3
D) Compilation Error
Question 5: Method Calling Hidden Variable
class Super {
    String name = "Super";
    void print() { System.out.println(name); }
}
class Sub extends Super {
    String name = "Sub";
}
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Super s = new Sub();
        s.print();
    }
}

A) Super
B) Sub
C) null
D) Compilation Error
